1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,967
Hi — this is Bella, for Chaitanya.

2
00:00:02,567 --> 00:00:06,393
This video is about how a student signs
in once and can open every

3
00:00:06,433 --> 00:00:08,133
textbook they're entitled to.

4
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:10,367
Every book is a separate site.

5
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:12,067
None of them has your password.

6
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:13,603
Start with the shape of it.

7
00:00:14,037 --> 00:00:17,103
There's no one big website with all the
textbooks inside.

8
00:00:17,737 --> 00:00:21,303
Each one is its own site, at its own
address, on its own server.

9
00:00:21,903 --> 00:00:23,837
And a student should only sign in once.

10
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:27,393
So how does a separate website know who
you are, and know you're allowed

11
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:28,133
in?

12
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:29,267
It has never seen you.

13
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:30,767
It doesn't have your password.

14
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:32,967
It has no idea who you are.

15
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:35,267
It needs something it can be handed at
the door.

16
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:36,667
A ticket.

17
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:39,257
When a student clicks a textbook, the
hub checks first:

18
00:00:39,823 --> 00:00:41,190
should this person be allowed in?

19
00:00:41,623 --> 00:00:43,123
If yes, it writes a ticket.

20
00:00:43,757 --> 00:00:46,923
Three things — who you are, which one
book, and when it expires.

21
00:00:47,457 --> 00:00:48,357
Twenty-four hours.

22
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:52,473
And it's signed — not with a signature
you could copy, but with a secret

23
00:00:52,513 --> 00:00:53,547
only the hub knows.

24
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:57,113
Change one character and the signature
stops matching.

25
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,613
Then the ticket is handed to the
textbook.

26
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:01,387
Here's the surprising part.

27
00:01:01,887 --> 00:01:04,420
The textbook doesn't trust that ticket
on its own.

28
00:01:04,987 --> 00:01:09,047
It can't — it doesn't know the secret,
so it can't tell a real signature

29
00:01:09,087 --> 00:01:10,153
from a forged one.

30
00:01:10,753 --> 00:01:13,320
Holding a ticket and checking one are
different powers.

31
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:15,007
So it asks the hub directly:

32
00:01:15,507 --> 00:01:18,973
is this ticket real, and is this person
allowed in my book?

33
00:01:19,573 --> 00:01:22,873
The hub runs six checks in order and
answers yes or no.

34
00:01:23,507 --> 00:01:27,133
Only then does the textbook let the
student in, and remember them for

35
00:01:27,173 --> 00:01:27,907
later pages.

36
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:29,797
Which leaves one hard problem.

37
00:01:30,330 --> 00:01:31,930
What happens when someone logs out?

38
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:33,897
The tickets are already out there.

39
00:01:34,563 --> 00:01:38,323
Each one is valid for twenty-four hours,
sitting on a site the hub doesn't

40
00:01:38,363 --> 00:01:39,063
control.

41
00:01:39,397 --> 00:01:42,697
You can't reach into a textbook site and
snatch a ticket back.

42
00:01:43,263 --> 00:01:45,863
Logging out of the hub, by itself,
changes nothing.

43
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:49,910
The obvious fix is a list of cancelled
tickets, checked every time.

44
00:01:50,643 --> 00:01:51,343
That works.

45
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:55,603
It also means keeping a list, keeping it
in sync everywhere, keeping it

46
00:01:55,643 --> 00:01:57,143
fast, and keeping it forever.

47
00:01:57,877 --> 00:02:00,543
You've traded one hard problem for a
permanent one.

48
00:02:01,077 --> 00:02:04,243
That is the moment to ask whether the
list is necessary at all.

49
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:05,937
This system does something simpler.

50
00:02:06,503 --> 00:02:08,637
When you log out, it writes down one
thing:

51
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:10,137
the time you logged out.

52
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:13,703
After that, any ticket issued before
that moment is dead.

53
00:02:14,303 --> 00:02:18,070
Not cancelled — just older than your
logout, so it no longer counts.

54
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:20,503
One timestamp instead of a list.

55
00:02:21,003 --> 00:02:25,103
Every outstanding ticket, everywhere,
invalid the instant you log out.

56
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:26,643
One Sign-In, Many Books.

57
00:02:27,177 --> 00:02:29,243
That's the mechanism, end to end.

58
00:02:29,877 --> 00:02:33,103
The full video adds the recap and a
prompt you can run on your own

59
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:33,843
service.

60
00:02:34,143 --> 00:02:35,277
The link is right below.
